          %h2#idea Idea.
          %p
            %strong Progress graphs
            are a simple model for a certain aspect of concurrency theory in Computer Science. The basic idea is to give a description of what can happen when several processes are modifying shared resources. Given a shared resource,
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_3.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_4.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            may be a shared variable, so a semaphore is used to ensure that only one process can write on it at a time. (Editing pages in the n-Lab is another example!)
          %p
            Our system will have
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_5.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            deterministic sequential processes
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_6.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_1, \ldots, Q_n
            Each will be abstracted as a sequence of ‘locks’ (denoted
            = succeed ")" do
              %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_7.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            = succeed ")" do
              %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_8.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_9.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            with suffices and superfixes to distinguish them), so
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_10.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Q
                      %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi R
                      %mn 1
                    %msubsup
                      %mi a
                      %mi i
                      %mn 1
                    %mo .
                    %msup
                      %mi R
                      %mn 2
                    %msubsup
                      %mi a
                      %mi i
                      %mn 2
                    %mi …
                    %msup
                      %mi R
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi i
                    %msubsup
                      %mi a
                      %mi i
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_i = R^1a^1_i.R^2a^2_i\ldots R^{n_i}a^{n_i}_i
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_11.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is an occurrence of a
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_12.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            or a
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_13.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %p
            There is a natural way to understand the possible behaviours of the concurrent execution of these processes. We associate to each process a different coordinate direction in the topological space,
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_14.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_15.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            whose
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_16.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi i
                    %mi th
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i^{th}
            coordinate describes the state or ‘local time’ of the
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_17.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi i
                    %mi th
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i^{th}
            process.
          %p
            We assume that each process starts at (local time) 0 and finishes at (local time) 1; the
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_18.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            and
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_19.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            actions correspond to sequences of real numbers between 0 and 1, which reflect the order of the
            = succeed "s" do
              %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_20.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            = succeed "s." do
              %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_21.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_22.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0,\ldots,0)
            and the final state
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_23.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1,\ldots, 1)
          %p
            To look at a particular example more closely, we suppose
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_24.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
