          %p
            The shaded area in the picture represents those states that correspond to ‘mutual exclusion’. Suppose we look at a state
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_27.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x,y)
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_28.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Pb
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi x
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi Vb
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Pb\lt x\lt Vb
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_29.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Pb
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi y
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi Vb
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Pb\lt y\lt Vb
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_30.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_1
            and
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_31.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_2
            have acquired
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_32.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            and not yet relinquished it, which is impossible since
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_33.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            can only be viewed by at most on process at any time. Such states are in the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong forbidden region
          %h2#formalising_programs
            Formalising
            = succeed "-programs" do
              %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_34.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi PV
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PV
          %p
            The PV language was introduced in 1968 by
            %span.newWikiWord
              E. W. Dijkstra
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/E.+W.+Dijkstra"} ?
            as an example of a toy language allowing concurrent execution of sequential processes . The PV language offers only two instructions called
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_35.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {P}
            and
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_36.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {V}
            as abbreviations for the Dutch terms
            = succeed "," do
              %em Prolaag
            = succeed "," do
              %em probeer te verlagen
            %em Verhogen
            (“increase”).
          %p
            Let
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_37.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            be a set whose elements are called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em semaphores
            %em s
            is associated with an
            %em arity
            that is an integer
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_38.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi s
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_s\geq 2
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_39.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha  \geq 2
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_40.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_41.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {P}(s)
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_42.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {V}(s)
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_43.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            is some semaphore. The terms,
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_44.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℙ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}
            = succeed "," do
              %em processes
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_45.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℙ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}
            is a process and
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_46.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_47.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℙ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_48.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℙ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}(j )
            the
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_49.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi j
                    %mi th
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j^{th}
            instruction of the process, in particular
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_50.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℙ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}(1)
            is the first instruction.
          %p We will separate the instructions of a process by a dot, mostly in order to make them easier to read. For example we have the processes
          .maruku-equation
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_51.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {P}(a).{V}(a)
          .maruku-equation
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_52.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(a).P(b).V(a).V(b)
          %p as we discussed more informally earlier.
          %h2#formalising_programs_and_their_semantics
            Formalising
            = succeed "-programs" do
              %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_53.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi PV
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PV
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong PV program
              is a finite sequence of processes separated by the operator
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_54.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mid
              = succeed "." do
                %em run concurrently with
          %p
            For instance,
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_55.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {P}(a).{V}(a) | {P}(a).{V}(a)
            is an example of a PV program made of two copies of a process, whilst
          .maruku-equation
            %math#mathml_4d2d8b42f8d091b0a67f871f3adb26722b328e2f_56.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {P}(a).{P}(b).{V}(a).{V}(b) | {P}(b).{P}(a).{V}(b).{V}(a)
          %p is an example made of two distinct processes.
          %p (To be continued.)
